      `Dawud (peace be upon him) is venerated in the Holy Quran as both an illustrious prophet and a righteous, just king upon the earth. Given the divine scripture of the Zabur (Psalms), Dawud combined unmatched martial valor, profound spiritual devotion, prophetic wisdom, and equitable state governance. The Quran introduces his heroism in the context of the faithful army led by Talut (Saul) facing the Philistine tyrant Jalut (Goliath). While many soldiers shrank in fear, young Dawud stepped forward in the name of Allah, slew Jalut with his sling, and broke the enemy forces: "So they defeated them by permission of Allah, and Dawud killed Jalut, and Allah gave him the kingship and wisdom and taught him from that which He willed" (Surah Al-Baqarah 2:251).`,
      `Allah blessed Dawud with extraordinary spiritual and physical gifts. He was endowed with a voice of such celestial resonance and melodious beauty that when he recited the praises of Allah from the Zabur, wild birds would suspend their flight and congregate above him, and the towering mountains joined in choral glorification: "Indeed, We subjected the mountains [to praise] with him, exalting [Allah] in the late afternoon and [after] sunrise. And the birds were assembled, all with him repeating [praises]" (Surah Sad 38:18–19). In hadith, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ praised the melodious voice of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari by saying he had been given one of the musical pipes (mizmar) of the family of Dawud.`,
      `Dawud was granted miraculous mastery over iron. Without fire or hammer, iron softened like malleable wax in his hands (Alanna lahul-hadid). Allah commanded him to fashion interconnected coats of ring mail armor (Dir') that protected warriors from blade and spear thrusts while allowing full bodily mobility, establishing early defense manufacturing in his state: "And We taught him the making of coats of armor for you to protect you from each other's battle violence; then will you be grateful?" (Surah Al-Anbiya 21:80). Despite reigning over a vast, prosperous kingdom, Dawud refused to consume food from the royal treasury, insisting upon eating strictly from what his own hands earned through artisan labor.`,
      `His personal regimen of worship is celebrated by Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as the dearest fasting and prayer to Allah. Dawud would sleep half the night, pray for a third, and sleep the remaining sixth, rising refreshed for dawn. He fasted alternately—one day fasting and one day eating—a spiritual discipline representing the absolute peak of voluntary devotion. In the Quran, Allah praises him with the honorific Awwab: "Remember Our servant Dawud, the possessor of strength; indeed, he was one who repeatedly turned back [to Allah]" (Surah Sad 38:17).`,
      `The Quran highlights his commitment to absolute justice. When two litigants miraculously climbed into his private prayer sanctuary (Mihrab) to present a dispute concerning ninety-nine ewes versus a single ewe, Dawud delivered an initial judgment, immediately realized it was a divine trial, and fell prostrate in repentance: "And Dawud became certain that We had tested him, and he asked forgiveness of his Lord and fell down bowing [in prostration] and turned in repentance" (38:24). Allah forgave him and confirmed his divine commission: "O Dawud, indeed We have made you a successor upon the earth, so judge between the people in truth and do not follow [your own] desire, as it will lead you astray from the way of Allah" (38:26). Islamic orthodoxy rejects Biblical fables claiming Dawud committed adultery or murdered his general Uriah, affirming the absolute infallibility ('Ismah) of prophets from major transgressions.`
