      `Sulayman (peace be upon him), the son and royal successor of Dawud, was endowed by Allah with an empire of unprecedented splendor, wisdom, and miraculous mastery over the natural and unseen realms. The Holy Quran commemorates him as a prophet-king who directed boundless worldly power entirely in the service of Tawhid and righteous stewardship: "And to Dawud We gave Sulayman. An excellent servant, indeed he was one who repeatedly turned back [to Allah]" (Surah Sad 38:30).`,
      `Sulayman prayed a famous supplication: "My Lord, forgive me and grant me a kingdom such as will not belong to anyone after me. Indeed, You are the Bestower" (Surah Sad 38:35). Allah answered his prayer with astonishing miracles. The wind was subjugated to his command, blowing gently at his order and traveling the distance of a month\'s journey in the morning and a month\'s journey in the evening (Surah Saba 34:12). Subservient legions of Jinn were placed beneath his authority—working under his direction to build grand sanctuaries, artistic arches, massive basins like reservoirs, and immovable cooking caldrons. A spring of molten copper (Ayn al-Qitr) was caused to flow for him, enabling unprecedented engineering and metallurgical construction.`,
      `Sulayman was granted comprehension of the speech of birds and animals (Mantiq at-Tayr). Marching with his multifaceted legions of jinn, men, and birds through the Valley of the Ants, Sulayman heard an ant warn her fellow colony: "O ants, enter your dwellings that you not be crushed by Sulayman and his soldiers while they perceive not" (Surah An-Naml 27:18). Sulayman smiled with joy, turning immediately to Allah in gratitude: "My Lord, enable me to be grateful for Your favor which You have bestowed upon me and upon my parents and to do righteousness of which You approve, and admit me by Your mercy into [the ranks of] Your righteous servants" (27:19).`,
      `His diplomatic and missionary engagement with Bilqis, the Queen of Sheba (Saba), highlights his devotion to Tawhid. Informed by the hoopoe bird (Hudhud) of a wealthy southern kingdom worshiping the sun, Sulayman dispatched a letter beginning with the majestic formula: "In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful: Be you not haughty against me, but come to me in submission [as Muslims]" (27:30–31). When the Queen sent extravagant royal gifts to test his intentions, Sulayman rejected worldly bribes, demanding their monotheistic submission. Before her arrival in Jerusalem, a scholar endowed with knowledge of the Scripture brought her royal throne across hundreds of miles in the blink of an eye. Upon witnessing the throne and entering Sulayman\'s magnificent palace with its floor of crystal clear glass over flowing water, the Queen recognized truth: "My Lord, indeed I have wronged myself, and I submit with Sulayman to Allah, Lord of the worlds" (27:44).`,
      `The Quran completely exonerates Sulayman from the slander of witchcraft and apostasy that later corrupted Jewish and occult lore: "Sulayman did not disbelieve, but the devils disbelieved, teaching people magic" (Surah Al-Baqarah 2:102). His passing was as miraculous as his life: standing leaned upon his staff in deep prayer, his soul departed without physical decay, while the subjugated Jinn continued their exhausting labor, unaware of his death until a termite gnawed through his wooden staff, causing his body to fall. This revealed to all that the Jinn possess zero knowledge of the unseen (Ghayb), dismantling human superstitions concerning demonic omniscience.`
