      `Ilyas (peace be upon him) is venerated in the Holy Quran as one of the noble messengers sent to Banu Israel to eradicate idol worship and restore pure monotheism. He is mentioned in Surah Al-An'am among the righteous lineage of Ibrahim: "And Zakariyya and Yahya and Isa and Ilyas—all were of the righteous" (6:85), and his mission is specifically detailed in Surah As-Saffat: "And indeed, Ilyas was from among the messengers" (37:123).`,
      `Classical historians and genealogists, including Ibn Kathir and At-Tabari, trace his lineage back to Harun ibn Imran. He was sent to the people of the northern region of the Levant—identified by many classical commentators with the city of Baalbek in modern Lebanon—during a period of catastrophic moral decay when the ruler and populace had abandoned the Torah to worship a Phoenician pagan idol known as Ba'l (Baal).`,
      `Ilyas boldly confronted his people and their idolatrous king, condemning the absurdity of worshiping an inanimate statue while abandoning the Creator who fashioned the universe: "Do you call upon Ba'l and leave the best of creators—Allah, your Lord and the Lord of your first forefathers?" (Surah As-Saffat 37:125–126). His preaching exposed the irrationality of pagan polytheism, demonstrating that true blessing, rain, and sustenance proceed solely from Allah, not pagan fertility gods.`,
      `The ruling elite rejected Ilyas with violent contempt, threatening his life and forcing him into seclusion in the mountains and caves. Classical reports narrate that as retribution for their stubborn defiance, Allah withheld rain from their lands for three continuous years, causing catastrophic drought and famine. When their pagan priests proved powerless to bring rain, the king summoned Ilyas, who called upon Allah alone, after which life-giving rains poured down, vindicating the monotheistic truth.`,
      `Despite witnessing undeniable signs, the majority of his people reverted to their corrupt ways, earning divine condemnation: "So they denied him, and indeed, they will be brought [to punishment], except the chosen servants of Allah" (37:127–128). Allah immortalized his righteous memory for all successive generations: "Peace be upon Ilyas! Indeed, We thus reward the doers of good. Indeed, he was of Our believing servants" (37:130–132).`
